WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.440
Model splitting can distribute weights across devices so a larger model fits.

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:07.320
Layer splitting assigns groups of layers to different GPUs.

00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:10.960
Other parallel methods divide operations more tightly and may require frequent

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:12.375
communication.

00:00:12.375 --> 00:00:13.560
Independent placement can be simpler:

00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:17.395
one GPU serves a general model while another serves code, vision, embeddings,

00:00:17.395 --> 00:00:19.230
or a second user queue.

00:00:19.230 --> 00:00:21.220
Failure and performance remain more isolated,

00:00:21.220 --> 00:00:23.560
but each workload must fit its assigned device.

00:00:23.560 --> 00:00:27.110
Serving multiple users often benefits from replicas or request routing rather than

00:00:27.110 --> 00:00:28.600
splitting every request.

00:00:28.600 --> 00:00:32.435
This can increase throughput and availability while preserving predictable single-GPU

00:00:32.435 --> 00:00:33.100
behavior.

00:00:33.100 --> 00:00:36.440
It also requires queueing, load balancing, and capacity controls.

00:00:36.440 --> 00:00:40.540
Batch workloads can process several documents, images, or prompts in parallel.

00:00:40.540 --> 00:00:44.095
Vision-and-text pipelines may assign image processing, language generation,

00:00:44.095 --> 00:00:47.060
and post-processing according to device capability and memory.

00:00:47.060 --> 00:00:50.915
Tradeoffs include motherboard and chassis constraints, power, heat, fan noise,

00:00:50.915 --> 00:00:55.300
purchase cost, idle consumption, software support, driver compatibility, monitoring,

00:00:55.300 --> 00:00:56.355
and recovery.

00:00:56.355 --> 00:00:59.909
Adding a second GPU can expose a weak power supply or airflow design.

00:00:59.909 --> 00:01:04.430
Define success numerically: required model and context, users, requests per minute,

00:01:04.430 --> 00:01:08.210
latency, throughput, memory margin, power budget, uptime, and cost.

00:01:08.210 --> 00:01:09.255
Without that baseline,

00:01:09.255 --> 00:01:13.055
multi-GPU becomes an expensive experiment with no clear pass condition.

00:01:13.055 --> 00:01:15.820
Separate memory-capacity need from compute-throughput need.

00:01:15.820 --> 00:01:20.079
Adding VRAM through splitting may allow a model to run while leaving generation limited by

00:01:20.079 --> 00:01:20.581
transfers,

00:01:20.581 --> 00:01:23.480
synchronization, or the least capable participating device.
